54
00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:25.920
Fast fact number one, written by Steve Carell.

55
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:26.580
Mm-hmm.

56
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.260
So here's something that maybe a lot of people don't know.

57
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.640
Steve also co-wrote 40-Year-Old Virgin.

58
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.300
This is not the first thing that Steve has written.

59
00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.460
So he got to write this episode.

60
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.920
You know, he also wrote another episode of The Office, Season 4, Episode 7, Survivorman.

61
00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:41.540
Mm-hmm.

62
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.900
So we'll get to that one.

63
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:50.360
But I found an old article from the LA Times where Steve did an interview all about how

64
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.180
he came to be the writer of this Season 2 finale.

65
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.840
Lady, that is some good detective work.

66
00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:58.680
I love it.

67
00:02:58.760 --> 00:02:59.780
I was very proud of myself.

68
00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:00.280
All right.

69
00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:01.660
So here's how it went down.

70
00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:07.700
So Steve had the idea as a general episode that Michael should throw a casino night down

71
00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:08.340
in the warehouse.

72
00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:15.220
And he pitched that idea to Greg, and Greg said, oh, that would make a great setting for

73
00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.480
the finale episode.

74
00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.860
And then, I guess, remember when Steve had to fly to New York to do the Valentine's Day

75
00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:23.340
episode?

76
00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:23.700
Yeah.

77
00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:29.640
On the airplane, Steve and Greg were together, and on the way there and on the way back,

78
00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:30.520
they sat down.

79
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.500
They sort of plotted out together what the finale would be with this casino night idea.

80
00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:36.580
Isn't that incredible?

81
00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.540
And they're outlining this episode.

82
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:39.800
I love that.

83
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.500
And Greg said to Steve, why don't you write it?

84
00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:44.740
You should write it.

85
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:48.400
And Steve was like, okay, yeah, I mean, I think that's great.

86
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:53.020
But then, this is a really fun tidbit, Steve got cold feet.

87
00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:53.820
Oh.

88
00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:55.580
And he tried to get out of it.

89
00:03:56.300 --> 00:04:01.280
So I guess this was during the time when he was still doing Evan Almighty on the weekends.

90
00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:05.360
You know, he would shoot on our show, sometimes like four days a week.

91
00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:09.040
We would shoot him out in four days, and then he would fly, and on the weekends, he would

92
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:10.060
be doing Evan Almighty.

93
00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:19.300
And so he flew home, and he had one weekend to write this finale script, and his parents

94
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.560
were in town visiting him at his house.

95
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.640
And so he went into Greg's office, and he said, I just don't think I can do it justice.

96
00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:28.980
There's no way I can do it.

97
00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.400
I should get out of it.

98
00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:35.280
But Steve had this great quote where he said, I don't know, Greg managed to talk him into

99
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.040
doing it anyway.

100
00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.440
Like, Greg has a way where by the time he was leaving Greg's office, he was like, I'm

101
00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.460
writing this episode again.

102
00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.340
How did that happen?

103
00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:43.540
I should not get out of it.

104
00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.160
So that makes me smile because it's very Greg.

105
00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.140
Greg, you know, we've sort of all said that he was kind of like our dad.

106
00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:50.920
Yeah.

107
00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.400
Although he's not old enough to be our dad, obviously, but he had sort of that father

108
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:57.700
mentor thing of like, you'll figure it out.

109
00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:58.560
You're going to do it.

110
00:04:58.700 --> 00:04:59.420
It's going to be okay.

111
00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:00.300
And then you're like, wait, what?

112
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:00.780
It's going to be great.

113
00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:02.300
Wait, I can totally see that.

114
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.040
I came in here to tell you I'm not doing it.

115
00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:06.340
And now I'm writing it.

116
00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:06.740
Yeah.

117
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:09.100
But he did say he was really pumped.

118
00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:13.180
Like, that was the other part of it was that Greg really infused him with this

119
00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:13.860
enthusiasm.

120
00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.060
So he was really excited.

121
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:21.080
So what he did was he went home and he would write the script after his parents went to

122
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:21.540
bed.

123
00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:25.120
So he would stay up at night and write the script and he turned it in.

124
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:30.800
And he said, then the writing staff did a bunch of punch ups and like they usually do.

125
00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:31.180
Right.

126
00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:32.940
And that's how we got the script.

127
00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:33.680
Wow.

128
00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:35.140
Way to go, Steve.

129
00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:37.220
Way to like crazy, man.

130
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:38.420
I love that story.

131
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:39.120
All right.

132
00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:40.040
That was a good one.

133
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.780
Moving on to fast fact number two.

134
00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:44.260
This is a supersized episode.

135
00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:50.000
Greg said that he was doing an interview for the Chicago Tribune and he had mentioned in

136
00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:55.000
